 ## Background
-CSG on implicit surfaces was formalized following Ricci's work in 1974, which established that minimum and maximum operations on functions result in union and intersection operations on their implicit surfaces [S2]. The method became widely popularized in real-time graphics through the "sphere tracing" technique, which demonstrated how SDFs could be used for antialiased ray tracing of implicit surfaces [S2]. The specific arithmetic formulations for SDFs (min for union, max for intersection) were further detailed in subsequent literature on geometric methods for ray tracing [S2]. The technique is a cornerstone of ray-marched rendering, enabling the creation of complex fractals and composite objects with minimal code overhead [S4, unofficial].
